      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Finishing" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "You will have six tails: the colour A magic ring tail and fasten-off tail, the colour B join tail and fasten-off tail, and the colour C join tail and fasten-off tail. Weave each through 3 to 4 stitches on the wrong side and trim. Steam-block flat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Joining" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Join the same way as any four-round granny: whip-stitch through back loops at corner ch-2 spaces and along side ch-1 spaces, or use a slip-stitch flat join on the wrong side. When colour C is the border colour on every square, the joins effectively disappear into the outer round."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Care" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water, or machine-wash on a delicates cycle. Check that all three colours are colourfast — especially important if you are mixing yarn brands from your remnant stash. Lay flat to dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What to try next" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The two-colour version is structurally identical but splits the rounds 2:2 rather than 1:2:1. The five-round version gives more room for colour banding — try one colour per round for a striped concentric effect, or keep the outer two rounds in a single joining colour for a consistent blanket border."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
